WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.180
Say you're throwing a dinner party
and you want to have a nice mix.

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:16.050
If you want there to be at least three
guests who know each other or at least

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:17.130
three who are strangers,

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.470
how many people do you need to invite?

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:24.240
The solution to this problem can be defined by what's called a Ramsey number.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.650
Ramsey numbers are central to the
field of math called graph theory.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:33.750
Graph theory is a study of networks.
Basically the question you ask is,

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.927
if you give me a certain system and maybe

00:00:36.927 --> 00:00:38.139
you give me some information about the system,

00:00:38.139 --> 00:00:39.840
what patterns can I expect?

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.490
Finding Ramsey numbers for networks

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:45.714
is one of the hardest problems in all of graph theory.

00:00:45.714 --> 00:00:47.640
This year, in a surprise announcement...

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:50.430
I didn't know they were working
on this before they invited me in,

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.730
so it was like a secret
for we didn't tell anyone.

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:57.930
An international group of researchers
revealed they made a major breakthrough on

00:00:57.930 --> 00:00:59.250
the Ramsey Number Problem.

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:03.150
Networks can be abstracted as graphs,

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:07.260
basic structures consisting of
collections of points called vertices,

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.630
connected by lines called edges.

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:14.310
Studying patterns in graphs can reveal
the underlying structures of complicated

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:19.020
systems like computer networks or
assist in problems like airline route

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.243
optimization.

00:01:20.243 --> 00:01:22.200
When considering large graphs,

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:27.060
mathematicians like to ask: When will
a pattern of a given size emerge?

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.790
This is where the Ramsey number comes in.

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:35.070
It's in some sense this threshold
between order on the one hand and

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:36.030
randomness on the other.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:41.250
Take a complete graph of N nodes where all the vertices are connected by edges.

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:44.070
Start coloring edges either red or blue.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:50.009
The Ramsey number R of K is the point
when structures or clique are forced to emerge.

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:54.180
The solution to the party
problem turns out to be six guests,

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:57.375
which means the Ramsey number of
three is six.

00:01:57.375 --> 00:01:59.190
With six guests,

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:04.496
you are guaranteed to have a set or
clique of three people who are either all friends

00:02:04.496 --> 00:02:06.224
or all strangers.

00:02:06.224 --> 00:02:08.850
What about the Ramsey number of four?

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.050
The answer is 18, but from
here on it gets complicated.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.577
We don't actually even know what the
fifth Ramsey number is.

00:02:15.577 --> 00:02:21.090
But for us, really the main and most
exciting thing is the

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:24.720
growth. So as K grows, how
does the Ramsey number grow?

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:28.740
In 1926, English mathematician,

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:33.863
Frank Ramsey introduced Ramsey numbers
in a paper published just weeks before he died.

00:02:33.863 --> 00:02:37.860
He showed the Ramsey number
for any finite number is finite.

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:41.700
Five years later, Hungarian
mathematician Paul Erdős,

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:46.200
who famously proposed hundreds of
math problems alongside George Szekeres,

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:49.591
found an upper bound or limit for
the Ramsey number.

00:02:49.591 --> 00:02:52.710
12 years later,  Erdős identified a lower bound.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.370
They had bounds and they said
it's between this and that,

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:02.230
but they didn't know the right answer.
They didn't know the exact numbers.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:04.690
Somehow everybody wanted to
know what's the right number.

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:08.350
Someone amazingly, despite the interest
and importance of this problem,

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:11.410
I mean there's an exponential
gap between these two bounds.

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:15.970
Decades later, mathematicians,
Julian Sahasrabudhe,

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:20.170
Rob Morris and Simon Griffiths
decided to tackle the problem.

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:22.090
So it just seems like there's a lot there.

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:28.558
But there's sort of some important
mystery hidden inside of this problem.

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:34.383
The team's first approach used a technique
from Erdős and Szekeres' first bound proof.

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:39.346
The method sorts complete colored
graphs in order to identify large cliques inside.

00:03:39.346 --> 00:03:41.077
It works like this.

00:03:41.077 --> 00:03:42.610
Start with any node.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:44.629
Look at the connecting edges.

00:03:44.629 --> 00:03:46.480
If there are more red ones,

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:48.520
then delete all the blue edges.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:53.110
Put the original choice in the red bucket
while still connected to the graph.

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:56.440
Put nodes with more blue
edges in the blue bucket.

00:03:56.830 --> 00:03:59.320
Repeat until everything is sorted.

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:03.370
To improve the efficiency of
their clique sorting strategy,

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:06.040
the team turned to a tool called a book.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:07.990
The book, it just says,

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:10.870
I want to try to take a bunch
of red steps in the beginning.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:14.590
By pulling out the monochromatic
clique called the spine,

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:18.820
you can speed up the search for a
large click with two easier searches.

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:22.150
It seemed that we had a viable
approach fairly early on,

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:25.210
and it was just that, 

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:30.750
we just need this one piece to make
it all come together and it will all work.

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:35.182
We thought, okay, maybe in
the first year we proved the piece, it was all fine,

00:04:35.182 --> 00:04:36.970
but then later we collapsed.

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:38.680
In 2021,

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:43.150
graduate student Marcelo Campos joined
the group to jumpstart the effort.

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:48.280
When I came in, they were working
on it for three years already,

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:53.733
and we worked on it for two
more years until we go to a solution.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:57.010
Eventually, the team tried their
techniques on a different problem,

00:04:57.370 --> 00:05:00.640
a set of numbers called Off-Diagonal Ramsey numbers.

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:05.560
Basically what happens is we relate it
to a very far off-diagonal Ramsey number,

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:11.170
and our algorithm actually works
better on the off-diagonal.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:15.280
This insight then led to an improved
solution for Ramsey numbers.

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:18.910
By March, the team was ready
to share their work publicly.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:22.210
At first, we were just going to post the
paper on arXiv, but then we thought,

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:24.936
okay, it might be nice to actually
do some lectures.

00:05:24.936 --> 00:05:29.167
And they all had very cryptic names, 

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:33.580
"Recent Advances in Ramsey Theory" or something like that.

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:38.380
In three coordinated lectures across
the globe from Oxford to Sao Paulo,

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:40.417
the team revealed their breakthrough.

00:05:40.417 --> 00:05:44.980
I mean news spread like wildfire. By
the time I walked out of the seminar,

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:47.470
my phone was blowing up.

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:51.490
The researchers succeeded in reducing
the known upper bound of Ramsey numbers

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:52.510
exponentially.

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:54.760
After 80 years of trying,

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:57.290
this is the first improvement
of this order of magnitude,

00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:59.030
which is a very big deal.

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:00.830
So we have a new tool.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:05.600
We developed this new algorithm
for constructing books,

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:09.830
but we don't really know what
other things it can be used for.

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:13.810
I think everybody is now trying to see
what the technique does to their favorite problem.

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:15.654
That's what Feynman said,

00:06:15.654 --> 00:06:19.520
every time I hear of a new idea, I try it on my three favorite problems.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:26.240
Have a look at these tiles.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:30.710
Can you tell whether any of these shapes
might only fill a plane to infinity by

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:31.742
never repeating?

00:06:31.742 --> 00:06:36.410
For more than half a century, mathematicians have sought
a tile with this property,

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:39.287
what's called an aperiodic monotile.

00:06:39.287 --> 00:06:44.840
This year, a tiling enthusiast together with a team
of researchers published a remarkable

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:46.310
proof of it's existence.

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:49.070
There's no reason why this
miracle should have happened.

00:06:49.610 --> 00:06:51.380
It's all a bit of a blur to be honest.

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:56.960
Intricate tile patterns go back millennia
from Roman mosaics to the geometric

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:58.823
patterns of Islamic palaces.

00:06:58.823 --> 00:07:03.440
For researchers, the study of tiling theory is more
than just an artistic pursuit.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:07.490
Tilings are a good model
for crystal structure,

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:11.600
the atomic structure of
crystals and quasi crystals.

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:16.520
But the mathematics that leads
to the monotile comes out of

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:18.140
the philosophy of logic.

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:22.640
The simplest tile patterns are ones
that repeat like a checkerboard.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:24.230
They're called periodic tilings.

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:29.450
But it's also possible to take this same
set of two tiles and create patterns

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:30.480
that never repeat.

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:32.810
This is called non-periodic tiling.

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:34.940
But you could ask,

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:39.740
could I have a set of tiles that
could make tilings of the plane,

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:42.170
but when they made the tilings,

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:45.560
those tilings were guaranteed
to be non-periodic.

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:49.550
Infinitely tiling sets like this
were repetition is impossible,

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:51.044
are called aperiodic.

00:07:51.044 --> 00:07:53.810
In the 1960s,

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:55.867
graduate student, Robert Berger found

00:07:55.867 --> 00:07:58.508
a tile set of more than 20,000 shapes

00:07:58.508 --> 00:08:02.023
that could be combined to tile a plane, aperiodically.

00:08:02.023 --> 00:08:05.420
His work set off a
race to find an even smaller set.

00:08:06.290 --> 00:08:10.460
By the 1970s Nobel Prize-winning
physicist and mathematician,

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:11.777
Sir Roger Penrose

00:08:11.777 --> 00:08:16.360
had discovered aperiodic tiling sets consisting of only two shapes.

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:19.280
The most famous of
which are the kite and dart.

00:08:19.910 --> 00:08:23.780
At the time, mathematicians wondered
if it was possible to go even further.

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:27.230
It was natural to ask if you
could get down to one shape.

00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:29.360
But nobody knew how to do that,

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:32.240
and nobody knew how to prove
that it was impossible.

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:37.310
This elusive shape was playfully
named an 'Einstein' tile from a German

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:39.650
pun of the phrase for 'one piece.'

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:45.410
The question of whether an Einstein tile
existed went unanswered for more than

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:48.230
50 years until David Smith,

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:52.010
a hobbyist interested in puzzles
and tiling entered the picture.

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:56.730
Dave was just engaged in an
open-ended exploration of

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:59.850
shapes and the kinds of interesting
tilings they could create.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:03.210
I've experimented with hundreds of shapes.

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:06.870
While playing around with a software
tool called PolyForm Puzzlesolver.

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:10.740
Smith stumbled onto one
especially interesting shape.

00:09:11.530 --> 00:09:14.430
I thought, that's odd. I've not
seen anything like that before.

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:18.210
And then I went on to cut out some shapes.

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:23.370
Dave glued the shapes together in larger
and larger patterns until he hit the

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:25.500
limit of what he could do by hand.

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:31.419
Usually what happens
is you find a periodic pattern early on.

00:09:31.419 --> 00:09:34.680
But with this I didn't. But I didn't rule it out.

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:39.030
It was at this point he contacted
computer scientist Craig Kaplan,

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:41.160
who creates pattern building software.

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:46.710
He said, can your software give us larger
and larger patches of this shape

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:51.390
so that we can see whether the surrounding
process eventually peters out or

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:53.340
whether it looks like we
can keep going forever?

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:56.970
The shape Smith discovered can
be constructed by subdividing,

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:59.056
a honeycomb grid of hexagons.

00:09:59.056 --> 00:10:03.444
First connect the midpoints of the opposite sides of the hexagons.

00:10:03.444 --> 00:10:06.450
This divides the hexagons into six smaller kites.

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:12.053
Smith's shape is made up of eight
adjacent kites combined from neighboring hexagons.

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:14.952
The pair named the new shape
the 'hat' tile.

00:10:14.952 --> 00:10:16.560
With his software.

00:10:16.710 --> 00:10:21.803
Kaplan constructed concentric rings
of the hat tile finding no repeating patterns.

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:25.470
I was able very quickly with
my software to go like, okay,

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:28.890
10 layers of surrounds, 12, 16.

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:30.960
It was around then that Dave said:

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:34.980
Could this be an answer to the
so-called Einstein problem?

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:37.830
Kaplan and Smith knew
they were onto something,

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:42.060
but to prove that the hat tile
was in fact an aperiodic monotile,

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:43.800
they needed some assistance.

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:47.203
So they reached out to others in the
tiling community,

00:10:47.203 --> 00:10:49.590
mathematician Chaim Goodman-Strauss,

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:53.220
and software engineer
Joseph Myers who took it from there.

00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:56.160
In just over a week, they had a solution.

00:10:56.910 --> 00:11:01.740
We've got to credit Joseph for bringing
together the rest of the proof because

00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:05.250
he, in record time, he just
put all the pieces in order,

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:09.000
got it all lined up and said
QED. It's true, it works.

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:13.950
Their proof involved piecing together
tiles into recursively larger versions of

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:14.693
themselves

00:11:14.693 --> 00:11:16.121
like this.

00:11:16.121 --> 00:11:20.010
First, take the hat tile shape and
create four metatiles named

00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:22.590
H, T, P, F.

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:27.510
These four metatiles can be compiled
into four larger versions of themselves

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:28.830
called supertiles.

00:11:29.190 --> 00:11:33.390
Then these four supertiles can be
combined into even larger supertiles,

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:36.225
eventually tiling the whole plane.

00:11:36.225 --> 00:11:39.600
Remarkably, while the researchers were
working on this proof,

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:42.030
Smith made another surprising discovery.

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:47.670
He sent me a second shape, one that
we eventually called the 'turtle'.

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:52.020
And he said, his email was

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:56.530
amusingly apologetic. I'm sorry about this,
but I think I have another one?

00:11:56.950 --> 00:12:01.330
An analysis of the turtle shape by
Myers led to a remarkable revelation.

00:12:01.810 --> 00:12:05.800
Joseph stunned us by saying,
the turtle is also aperiodic,

00:12:06.220 --> 00:12:10.420
and then he said, in fact, the turtle
and the hat are part of a continuum,

00:12:10.780 --> 00:12:15.550
and all the shapes in that continuum
are aperiodic and tile in the same way.

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:18.460
At either end of this continuum lie,

00:12:18.460 --> 00:12:21.820
two unique tiles named
the chevron and the comet,

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:23.977
both of which are periodic.

00:12:23.977 --> 00:12:25.240
In between these outliers,

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:30.157
the hat and the turtle shapes fall on
a continuum of an infinite set of 13-sided,

00:12:30.157 --> 00:12:31.703
aperiodic tiles.

00:12:31.703 --> 00:12:34.300
News of this discovery spread quickly.

00:12:34.750 --> 00:12:39.370
This particular paper is probably
one of the most heavily peer-reviewed

00:12:39.370 --> 00:12:41.470
papers in existence.

00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:45.280
We all downloaded it and
we all read it immediately.

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:49.570
But amid all the attention came a
specific criticism of their breakthrough.

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:53.680
After we put our hat
paper online in March,

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:57.160
there was one objection to it that
was raised more than any other.

00:12:57.700 --> 00:13:00.430
About one seventh of the
hats had to be flipped over.

00:13:00.430 --> 00:13:03.850
To some, the two reflections
of this one tile were,

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:07.002
in fact two different shapes and not a
monotile.

00:13:07.002 --> 00:13:08.590
Which begged the question:

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:14.373
Does there exist an aperiodic
monotile that never uses reflection?

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:19.690
That's a fantastic follow-up
question that we had no insight into.

00:13:20.740 --> 00:13:24.580
Astonishingly less than a week after
their initial paper was published,

00:13:24.730 --> 00:13:26.965
Smith discovered another new shape.

00:13:26.965 --> 00:13:31.800
I thought, well, it's got a lot
more freedom than the others,

00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:34.360
and all the sides are equal.

00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:36.040
Dave said, well, hang on.

00:13:36.460 --> 00:13:40.810
What if I force myself
to tile with that shape,

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:42.430
never using reflection.

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:47.170
This intriguing shape dubbed the 'spectre'
turned out to lie at the exact middle

00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:48.760
of the hat-tile continuum.

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:53.560
Another round of analysis led to a proof
that the spectre shape tiles the plane

00:13:53.590 --> 00:13:54.552
aperiodically

00:13:54.552 --> 00:13:55.957
without reflection.

00:13:55.957 --> 00:13:57.970
A true Einstein tile.

00:13:59.170 --> 00:14:01.960
Again, a miracle, no
reason to expect that,

00:14:01.960 --> 00:14:05.410
but just good luck that
Dave stumbled onto it.

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:08.230
It still is a bit of
a dream, to be honest.

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:11.350
Because I've always felt that

00:14:13.890 --> 00:14:18.290
I wanted to discover
something and it happened.

00:14:23.020 --> 00:14:25.960
Given a set of numbers,
say from one to a million,

00:14:26.350 --> 00:14:30.370
how many can you choose from so that
no three of them are equally spaced.

00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:33.970
If you choose too many of
them, and then unavoidably,

00:14:33.970 --> 00:14:36.730
there are some three of them
which are equally-spaced.

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:41.320
The study of number patterns like these
is a central pursuit of the field of

00:14:41.320 --> 00:14:42.760
additive combinatorics.

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:45.635
The biggest unsolved problem left at
the moment is to do with

00:14:45.635 --> 00:14:47.163
arithmetic progressions.

00:14:47.530 --> 00:14:50.860
So it came as a shock when two computer
scientists announced they'd made a

00:14:50.870 --> 00:14:54.680
significant breakthrough on what's
called the three arithmetic progression

00:14:54.680 --> 00:14:57.260
problem blowing past the previous record.

00:14:57.890 --> 00:15:02.510
It was a big surprise, and my immediate
reaction was, of course, skepticism.

00:15:03.740 --> 00:15:07.550
Graduate student, Zander Kelly,
and associate professor Raghu Meka,

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:09.560
didn't set out to find this proof.

00:15:10.070 --> 00:15:13.670
We were working on this problem
called parallel repetition,

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:16.550
which is this classical question in
theoretical computer science.

00:15:16.610 --> 00:15:18.710
We thought it had connections
to additive combinatorics.

00:15:19.460 --> 00:15:23.270
We started digging into it, and
that kind of led us to this problem.

00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:25.430
It's a pretty famous problem. I mean,

00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:29.060
certainly in pure math,

00:15:29.060 --> 00:15:33.290
but also it's well-known in theoretical
computer science because of the

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:34.910
techniques used to attack it.

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:36.920
An arithmetic progression of length

00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:40.480
three is just three numbers with the
same step in between them.

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:42.640
For example, 1, 2, 3,

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:44.784
or 3, 7, 11,

00:15:44.784 --> 00:15:46.940
or 5, 10, 15.

00:15:47.270 --> 00:15:50.600
So you just have three numbers, and
then one of them is in the middle,

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:53.390
and it's exactly in the middle.
It's the average of the other two.

00:15:53.990 --> 00:15:58.190
The three progression problem
dates back to 1936 to a paper by

00:15:58.190 --> 00:16:01.070
mathematicians Paul Erdős and Paul Turán.

00:16:01.400 --> 00:16:05.810
The pair wanted to know how many numbers
smaller than some ceiling N can be put

00:16:05.810 --> 00:16:08.900
in a set without creating
any three term progressions.

00:16:09.470 --> 00:16:10.820
In 1946,

00:16:11.060 --> 00:16:14.960
a method was found to construct sets
without producing these progressions,

00:16:15.230 --> 00:16:17.849
giving mathematicians a
floor to work with.

00:16:17.849 --> 00:16:19.340
In 1953,

00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:21.060
a ceiling was discovered,

00:16:21.060 --> 00:16:24.230
a threshold beyond which a set must inevitably contain

00:16:24.290 --> 00:16:25.639
a three-term progression.

00:16:25.639 --> 00:16:26.480
Since then,

00:16:26.510 --> 00:16:29.570
there has been only slow
progress on lowering this ceiling.

00:16:29.780 --> 00:16:31.640
As far as I know, the actual

00:16:33.350 --> 00:16:36.650
fundamental technique itself
seems to be suboptimal.

00:16:37.190 --> 00:16:41.150
Zander and Meka looked through the existing
body of work on the problem in search

00:16:41.150 --> 00:16:42.200
of new approaches.

00:16:42.530 --> 00:16:47.330
So the surprising thing I would say
is some of the tools, or the main

00:16:47.330 --> 00:16:50.240
set of tools, were there in previous works.

00:16:50.570 --> 00:16:54.973
The pair combined two tools that hadn't
been used together before on this problem.

00:16:55.070 --> 00:16:59.480
The density increment strategy focuses
on a structured window inside a set.

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:03.053
You find some subset of your set

00:17:03.053 --> 00:17:05.293
where you can zoom in on that subset.

00:17:05.450 --> 00:17:08.150
So you're shrinking the
universe of what you're looking at.

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:10.730
The density of the set has increased.

00:17:11.660 --> 00:17:12.160
We've made some progress in the sense
that it's always easier to find a

00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:17.090
three-progression in a denser set.

00:17:17.390 --> 00:17:19.420
An algorithmic procedure called sifting

00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:22.940
is used to find universes in which the set is dense.

00:17:23.390 --> 00:17:25.850
So basically you start off with this set
that you're trying to find three-term

00:17:25.850 --> 00:17:28.280
progressions in, and it
could look very chaotic.

00:17:28.520 --> 00:17:31.100
So there's sort of numbers
flying all over the place.

00:17:31.310 --> 00:17:36.140
It takes the whole set and it
shifts it by a random amount

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:39.440
in different directions, like a couple times.

00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:43.070
It sifts out the noise, right? You're
just left with this sort of kernel.

00:17:43.490 --> 00:17:47.930
We're able to find this hidden
structure planted inside.

00:17:48.290 --> 00:17:49.680
With these combined tools,

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:54.354
Kelley and Meka were able to dramatically
lower the ceiling on the three-progression problem.

00:17:54.354 --> 00:17:56.280
But as outsiders to the field,

00:17:56.340 --> 00:17:59.040
the pair sought input on
their paper before publishing.

00:17:59.250 --> 00:18:04.200
Just sent it to a couple people
to see if they wanted to take

00:18:04.210 --> 00:18:07.554
a look before we've released it publicly.

00:18:07.554 --> 00:18:10.013
It was just like a pretty big claim, so.

00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:14.340
Kelly sent his paper to mathematicians,
Thomas Bloom and Olaf Sisasks,

00:18:14.670 --> 00:18:16.740
the previous record
holders on the problem.

00:18:16.830 --> 00:18:21.180
Given the huge leap forward of the bound
that they were claiming, I thought,

00:18:21.180 --> 00:18:25.920
well, it can't be done that easily.

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:30.570
And then we basically spent the rest of
the day on a Zoom call going through the

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:32.520
paper, and by the end
of the day on Monday,

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:34.560
we were convinced that it was correct.

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:40.170
Yeah, so we're very excited about how
these methods could be useful for

00:18:40.170 --> 00:18:44.950
questions in, going back to theoretical
computer science.

00:18:44.950 --> 00:18:47.930
Questions in computational complexity,
finding better algorithms.

00:18:48.390 --> 00:18:50.880
But there've been so many cases
even in the past year or two,

00:18:51.240 --> 00:18:56.100
where out of nowhere there's this sort
of decades old problem that's just been

00:18:56.100 --> 00:18:57.750
sort of solved in a beautiful,

00:18:58.020 --> 00:18:59.436
fantastic way

00:18:59.436 --> 00:19:01.710
using tools that we thought
weren't strong enough, and they are.

